m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: To achieve prebiotic L-alanine synthesis, the network employs a diverse, environment-rationalized approach rooted in experimentally supported and chemically plausible routes. The main strategies combine Strecker-type aminonitrile formation/hydrolysis, transamination from \u03b1-ketoacids, reductive amination of pyruvate, formaldehyde\u2013acetaldehyde C\u2013C coupling, and UV/thermal aldehyde chemistry, using intermediates such as HCN, ammonia, pyruvate, acetaldehyde, and methylamine. All reactions are explicitly atom- and charge-balanced; water and byproducts are included where required. Multiple environmental contexts are utilized, from mineral-catalyzed hydrothermal synthesis to surface photochemistry and prebiotic pools, deliberately mapping interconnected alternative entry points for the synthesis of L-alanine. Ten chemically robust pathways are formulated, each fully specifying molecules, balanced reactions, justified minerals/catalysts, and appropriate geochemical logic.\n\nMOLECULES (16 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CH4 (methane) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2O (water) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_005: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_006: CH2O (formaldehyde) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: CH3CHO (acetaldehyde) [intermediate]\n  - mol_008: CH3CN (acetonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: CH3COCOOH (pyruvic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: CH3NH2 (methylamine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C3H7NO2 (L-alanine) [target]\n  - mol_012: CH3CH(NH2)CN (alanine aminonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: CH3CH(NH2)CHO (2-aminopropanal) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: CH3COOH (acetic acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_015: CH3CH(OH)CN (lactonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: CH3CH(OH)NH2 (2-aminopropanol) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (18 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_005, mol_004 (Methane and ammonia to hydrogen cyanide) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_002: mol_001, mol_004 -> mol_006 (Formaldehyde formation from CO2 reduction) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_003: mol_006, mol_002 -> mol_007, mol_004 (Acetaldehyde synthesis by aldol condensation of formaldehyde and methane derivatives) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_007, mol_001, mol_004 -> mol_009 (Pyruvate formation from acetaldehyde oxidation and CO2 addition) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_005: mol_009, mol_003 -> mol_011, mol_004 (Pyruvate reductive amination to alanine) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_006: mol_007, mol_003, mol_005 -> mol_012, mol_004 (Strecker synthesis of alanine aminonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_012, mol_004 -> mol_011 (Hydrolysis of alanine aminonitrile to L-alanine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_008, mol_004 -> mol_010 (Acetonitrile hydrolysis to amide then to methylamine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_010, mol_006, mol_003 -> mol_011, mol_004 (Methylamine to L-alanine via formose\u2013transamination sequence) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_010: mol_014, mol_003, mol_006 -> mol_011, mol_004 (Reductive amination of acetic acid to alanine) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_011: mol_005, mol_004 -> mol_008 (HCN trimerization and hydrolysis to acetonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_007, mol_005 -> mol_015 (Lactonitrile (2-hydroxypropanenitrile) format